WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.800
Often you need the depth at a time between high and low.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.075
For a quick answer, use the rule of twelfths.

3
00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:09.725
The tide doesn’t rise evenly.

4
00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:14.300
It starts slowly, runs fastest in the middle, and slows again.

5
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:19.950
Over six hours it rises one twelfth, two twelfths, three, three, two, and one.

6
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.725
Now work it.

7
00:00:21.975 --> 00:00:25.950
Low water is zero point five feet at six in the morning.

8
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.200
High water is six point five feet at noon.

9
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:32.350
That’s a range of six feet over six hours.

10
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.450
What about nine o’clock, three hours in?

11
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.425
Add the first three hours: one plus two plus three.

12
00:00:39.675 --> 00:00:41.375
Six twelfths.

13
00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:44.575
Six twelfths of six feet is three feet.

14
00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:50.250
So the height of tide at nine is zero point five plus three: about three and a half feet.

15
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.100
And if the chart shows a five-foot spot?

16
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.250
Five plus three and a half.

17
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.900
About eight and a half feet of water.

18
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.075
That’s lesson seven.

19
00:01:01.325 --> 00:01:04.625
Depth equals charted depth plus height of tide.

20
00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:07.350
Spring tides at new and full moon.

21
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.025
One, two, three, three, two, one.

22
00:01:10.275 --> 00:01:12.325
Next time: currents.
